# Copy to .env — .env is gitignored and must never be committed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database (required)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL connection. For local development, `docker compose up -d` starts
# a matching database (postgres/postgres, database personal_os_dev).
#
# In production DATABASE_URL may point at a connection pooler;
# DIRECT_DATABASE_URL must always be the direct (non-pooled) connection, which
# is what migrations use. Locally the two are simply identical.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/personal_os_dev"
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/personal_os_dev"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication (required for the hosted app)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sign-in is email + password held by this app itself. No Google Cloud, no
# OAuth client, no external identity provider, no credit card — nothing here
# requires a billing account anywhere.
#
# Registration is public self-serve: anyone can create an account at /signup
# (no allowlist, no setup token, no invite). Every account only ever sees
# its own data. "Forgot password" is covered by the recovery codes shown at
# sign-up — the app sends no email — with scripts/reset-password.mjs as the
# self-hoster's break-glass fallback.

# Session-signing secret. Generate one with:  npx auth secret   (or:
# openssl rand -base64 33). Rotating it signs everyone out immediately.
# Also keys the HMAC that pseudonymizes client addresses in the sign-up /
# sign-in / recovery rate-limit counters.
AUTH_SECRET=""

# OPTIONAL admin switch: set to "1" to pause NEW registrations (abuse
# incident, or running a private instance). Existing accounts sign in as
# normal. Leave unset for normal public operation.
# SIGNUPS_DISABLED="1"

# Required when self-hosting behind a proxy (Vercel sets this itself).
# AUTH_TRUST_HOST="true"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Health import upload size (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An Apple Health export never travels as a single request: the browser slices
# it into 4 MB parts, so a hosting platform's request-body cap (Vercel's is
# ~4.5 MB) does not bound the file. What bounds it is how much the deployment
# can reassemble and parse in one go — 2 GB self-hosted, and 256 MB on Vercel,
# where the function's scratch space and a free-tier database both apply.
#
# Set this to the real limit in megabytes when you know better than the
# defaults — a reverse proxy with its own client_max_body_size, or a plan and
# database that can hold more. It can never raise the app's own 2 GB ceiling.
#
# If you run behind a reverse proxy, make sure it accepts an 8 MB body: nginx's
# client_max_body_size defaults to 1 MB, which would refuse an upload part.
# HEALTH_MAX_UPLOAD_MB="512"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Online food search (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything below is optional. With none of it set the app still searches its
# bundled food table, your custom foods, your favourites, your recent foods and
# anything previously pulled from a provider and cached — which is what makes
# nutrition work offline.
#
# Only a search term or a public barcode is ever sent to these services. Your
# meals, goals, weight, schedule, habits and notes never leave the machine.

# USDA FoodData Central — the primary source for generic, unbranded foods
# ("chicken breast", "rolled oats").
#
#   1. Get a free key (no card): https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/api-key-signup.html
#   2. Paste it below and restart the dev server.
#
# Server-side only: this is read inside a `server-only` module and never reaches
# the browser bundle. Do not prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_, and do not commit a
# real key. Without it the app says so in the search results and carries on.
USDA_FDC_API_KEY=""

# Open Food Facts needs no key and is always available. It covers branded,
# packaged products and is used read-only — the app never writes or uploads to
# it. There is nothing to configure; this note exists so the absence of a
# variable is not mistaken for a missing step.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Background reminders — Web Push (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generate a key pair once with:  npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# Without these, reminders still work while a tab is open; with them (and a
# scheduled call to /api/reminders/run) reminders arrive as push
# notifications on devices that enabled them in Settings.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=""
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=""
# Contact for the push service, e.g. "mailto:you@example.com".
VAPID_SUBJECT=""

# Shared secret protecting the scheduled reminder endpoint. Every scheduler —
# Vercel Cron (daily on the free plan) or a free external one like
# cron-job.org for the real every-few-minutes cadence — must send it as
# "Authorization: Bearer <value>". See docs/web-push-setup.md.
CRON_SECRET=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI assistant — local Ollama (no variable needed)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The assistant has no environment configuration on purpose: the Ollama server
# URL, model and mode are per-account settings, edited in Settings → AI
# assistant. There is no cloud provider, no API key and no fallback — the only
# machine your data is ever sent to is the Ollama server you name there, which
# should be one you run yourself (this machine or one on your network).
# Setup and the full safety model: docs/ai-assistant.md.
